  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe switch from binary to real-valued representations to escape local optima found by binary optimizations. The existing binary representations create hard transitions that correlate strongly with shifted versions, leading to high overlaps. By using real values, we explicitly allow intermediate states between 0 and 1, which can reduce correlation at all shifts. We minimize the maximum shift overlap by combining adaptive grid resolution (from 256 to higher points) with a damped multistart local search on real-parameter individuals. The methodology leverages continuous optimization techniques to find configurations that satisfy the integral constraint while improving the overlap.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe optimization seeks to minimize:\nC5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nsubject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n\u222b h(x) dx = 1\n\nGrid handling and projection:\nFoundation at n=256 samples. For higher grids, we transform parent via interpolation. We maintain sum constraint using projection:\nsumvector = (0 \u2264 clip(simplex projection) \u2264 1) \u2768\nGradient computation: FFT-based for continuous representation\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Global population initialization:\n- 50 candidates via correlated sampling from Gaussian distributions on simplex\n- Each candidate vector has length n=256, sum=128\n- Initial sampling: X = np.random.normal(0.4,0.2, n), then simplex projection\n\n2. Local search phase per candidate:\n- Over 1000 steps:\n  - Compute gradient (FFT correlation)\n  - Project gradient onto constraint\n  - Update: h = clamp( h - learning_rate * gradient )\n  - Regularization: \u03bc = max(0, \u03bc - \u03c4)\n\n3. Adaptive termination:\n- Minimum improvement fails to drop C5 by 0.0005 per 100 steps\n- C5 improves each cycle or time threshold hits\n\nConstraint handling:\nIntegral constraint enforced by:\ndef project_simplex(v, s=sum):\n    y = np.ascontiguousarray(v, dtype=np.float64)\n    x = np.zeros(y.shape, dtype=np.float64)\n    # Use Frank-Wolfe projection\n\nObjective evaluation:\nC5 = max( np.correlate(h.astype(float), 0.1 - h.astype(float), 'valid') * (dx / 0.5)\ndx=2/256\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Initial population generates samples from normal/Gaussian distributions\n- Learning rate adapts with step number\n- Decay factor: 0.99995/sqrt(time)\n- Restart rule when gradient vanishes\n\nNumerical robustness:\n- Gradient computed via dq/dh_i using finite differences\n- Jacobian repair for gradient !=0\n- Use dtype=np.float64 for precision\n- Explicit fallback to parent if optimization fails\n\nRuntime safeguards:\n- 1100s budget tracked by wall clock\n- Termination if improvement stops for 100 steps\n- Return best solution ensuring:\n  - Sum is exactly 128\n  - Values in [0,1]\n  - C5 computed with validate_c5 function\n\nReturn contract:\nOutput format: (h_vector_opt, c5_val, n_points)\nn_points=256 (default) if grid unchanged\n\nThe optimization dynamically balances global exploration (population) with local refinement (gradient steps) and adapts its parameters based on progress to escape local optima.",
